WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:19.849
Hello. My name is Mohammad Ali Jaffry. I am an architect. With ten years of architectural experience. Welcome, to this episode. What this research is for.

2
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:34.382
Picture a city.

3
00:00:35.422 --> 00:00:41.378
A monolith is one department store: everything under one roof, one set of doors, one power supply.

4
00:00:41.758 --> 00:00:49.676
Microservices are a street of small shops, each with its own keys, its own till, its own opening hours, trading with each other across the street.

5
00:00:50.656 --> 00:00:54.696
Now suppose AI helps design, build, and run those shops.

6
00:00:55.076 --> 00:01:00.277
Did the layout of the street change — or only the tools in the builders’ hands?

7
00:01:01.317 --> 00:01:04.161
That is the question this research asks.

8
00:01:05.201 --> 00:01:15.743
The end product is a research article on how AI has changed microservice architecture: its design, its boundaries, its communication, its operations, and the tradeoffs that come with those changes.

9
00:01:16.723 --> 00:01:17.989
The method is plain.

10
00:01:19.029 --> 00:01:23.545
About two days of deep research, then an article written from the evidence gathered.

11
00:01:23.925 --> 00:01:28.755
Nothing in the article gets to be asserted that was not first found.

12
00:01:30.075 --> 00:01:31.677
And the order matters.

13
00:01:32.717 --> 00:01:40.345
The history of microservices, and the architectures and design patterns of that style, come first — they are the baseline.

14
00:01:41.385 --> 00:01:46.203
Only later do the chapters ask what AI changed in that baseline.

15
00:01:47.243 --> 00:01:53.710
You cannot measure a change without the ‘before’ picture, so the ‘before’ picture is drawn carefully, and it is drawn first.

16
00:01:54.690 --> 00:02:01.400
Each answer is saved as its own document, so the draft can be read offline while the research continues.

17
00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:05.403
This series follows the same shape: one episode per chapter.

18
00:02:06.383 --> 00:02:08.449
Three things to carry away.

19
00:02:09.489 --> 00:02:10.128
One.

20
00:02:11.168 --> 00:02:16.508
One question — how AI changed microservice architecture — under five headings.

21
00:02:17.208 --> 00:02:17.719
Two.

22
00:02:18.759 --> 00:02:23.775
History and patterns first, as the baseline; only then the AI question.

23
00:02:24.815 --> 00:02:25.569
Three.

24
00:02:26.609 --> 00:02:30.847
One document per answer; one episode per chapter.

25
00:02:32.167 --> 00:02:35.580
Next: what kind of software is Kubernetes?

26
00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:41.438
Four ways to sort software, and why the research lives on only one of them.
